      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the pork belly in a pan, cover with cold water. Add the peppercorns and a few slices of ginger. Bring to the boil, reduce to a simmer. Cook for 35 to 40 minutes until a chopstick pierces the meat easily. Remove and cool."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Refrigerate the pork for at least 30 minutes, or overnight. Cold pork slices cleanly without falling apart."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slice the cold pork belly as thinly as you can, about 3mm. It should be thin enough to curl when it hits the hot wok."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the sauce ingredients together in a small bowl."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the wok over high heat until smoking. Add the oil. Add the pork slices in a single layer. Press down and leave for 60 seconds until the fat starts to render and the edges colour. Toss."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, ginger, and chilli. Stir-fry for 30 seconds."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the cabbage and leek. Toss everything together. Pour in the sauce. Stir-fry for 1 to 2 minutes until the vegetables are just tender. Serve immediately with rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Pork slices do not curl and the fat is not rendered",
              "cause": "The slices were too thick or the pan not hot enough.",
              "fix": "Ensure slices are 3mm or thinner. Heat the wok until it smokes before adding any fat or pork."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Dish is very greasy",
              "cause": "The pork was too fatty and the fat was not drained after the first stage.",
              "fix": "After stir-frying the pork, tilt the wok and spoon off excess rendered fat before adding the vegetables."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hui guo rou is considered one of the most representative dishes of Sichuan home cooking. Unlike restaurant Sichuan food, which leans on complex preparations, this dish is what Sichuanese families eat on weeknights. The method of boiling then frying pork appears across Chinese regional cuisines, but the combination of doubanjiang, dark soy, and garlic sprouts is specific to Sichuan. Food historians trace the dish to a tradition of using leftover boiled pork from ritual meals."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
